        "content": "## Ministry of Environment and Water of Bulgaria - UNFCCC\nURL: https://unfccc.int/sites/default/files/resource/2024%20BG_BTR1.pdf\nTitle: 2024%20BG_BTR1.pdf\n\nURL Source: https://unfccc.int/sites/default/files/resource/2024%20BG_BTR1.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Thu, 16 Jan 2025 11:24:09 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 180\n\nMarkdown Content:\n1\n\n# FIRST BIENNIAL TRANSPERANNCY REPORT OF BULGARIA \n\n# under \n\n# the Paris Agreement \n\n# 12/31/2024 \n\n# Ministry of Environment and Water of Bulgaria 2\n\nBulgaria’s Biennial Transparency Report 2024 \n\n# Contents \n\n1.  INTRODUCTION ................................ ................................ ................................ ................................ ..  3\n\n2.  NATIONAL INVENTORY R EPORT  ................................ ................................ ................................ ....  4\n\n2.1.  INTRODUCTION ................................ ................................ ................................ ..............................  4\n\n2.2.  SUMMARY OF GHG EMISS IONS TRENDS  ................................ ................................ ....................  4\n\n2.3.  DESCRIPTION OF THE N ATIONAL INVENTORY AR RANGEMENTS  ................................ ......  14 \n\n3.  INFORMATION NECESSAR Y TO TRACK PROGRESS  ................................ ................................ ...  23 \n\n3.1.  NAT IONAL CIRCUMSTANCES  AND INSTITUTIONAL AR RANGEMENTS  ..............................  23 \n\nNATIONAL CIRCUMSTANCES  ................................ ................................ ................................ .................  23 \n\nINSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS  ................................ ................................ ................................ .......  47 \n\n3.2.  Description of the Nationally Determined Contribution  ................................ ................................ . 53 \n\n3.3.  Indicator, definitions, methodologies and progress  ..\n\n## Development of the National Sustainable Heating Roadmap for ...\nURL: https://energie.gov.md/sites/default/files/document/attachments/task_5_development_of_the_national_sustainable_heating_roadmap_for_moldova_0.pdf\nTitle: task_5_development_of_the_national_sustainable_heating_roadmap_for_moldova_0.pdf\n\nURL Source: https://energie.gov.md/sites/default/files/document/attachments/task_5_development_of_the_national_sustainable_heating_roadmap_for_moldova_0.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Mon, 11 Aug 2025 11:45:39 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 114\n\nMarkdown Content:\nTHE WORLD BANK GROUP \n\n# National Assessment of Potential \n\n# for Sustainable Heating and \n\n# Sustainable Heating Roadmap \n\n# for Moldova \n\n# TASK  5: Development of \n\n# the  National Sustainable \n\n# Heating Roadmap for \n\n# Moldova \n\n# June  202 5National Sustainable Heating Roadmap for Moldova Task 5 Report  \n\n> EXERGIA S.A. 1\n\nT\n\nPROJECT  CODE: 9 -1-23-09 \n\nFILE NAME  DATE  STATUS/ \n\nVERSION  PREPARED  CHECKED  APPROVED \n\nDevelopment of \n\nthe National \n\nSustainable \n\nHeating Roadmap \n\nfor Moldova \n\n20 .0 5.2025  V1.0  M. Kosir \n\nM. Briola \n\nK. Georgakopoulos \n\nG. Vlondakis  G. Vlondakis \n\nDevelopment of \n\nthe National \n\nSustainable \n\nHeating Roadmap \n\nfor Moldova \n\n13.06.202 6 V2.0  K. Georgakopoulos \n\nM Briola \n\nM. Kosir \n\nG. Vlondakis  G. Vlondakis \n\nDisclaimer : “The authors take full responsibility for the contents of this report. The \n\nopinions expressed do not necessarily reflect the view of the  World  Bank”. National Sustainable Heating Roadmap for Moldova Task 5 Report  \n\n> EXERGIA S.A. 2\n\n# PROJECT DATA \n\nProject Title:  The national assessment of potential for efficiency in heating \n\nand the heating roadmap for Moldova \n\nBeneficiary \n\ncountry: \n\nRepublic of  Moldova \n\nContracting \n\nAuthority \n\nWorld  Bank \n\nRoger  Coma Cunill , Senior Energy Specialist, \n\nrcomacunill@worldbank.org \n\nSylvia  Martinez Romero , Lead Energy Specialist, \n\nsmartinezromero@worldbank.org \n\nPromoter / \n\nBeneficiary \n\nMinistry of Energy \n\nNational Centre f\n\n## Energy Policy - IIASA PURE\nURL: https://pure.iiasa.ac.at/id/eprint/21038/1/1-s2.0-S0301421524005135-main.pdf\nTitle: Need for speed: Co-creating scenarios for climate neutral energy systems in Austria in 2040\n\nURL Source: https://pure.iiasa.ac.at/id/eprint/21038/1/1-s2.0-S0301421524005135-main.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Wed, 03 Dec 2025 15:36:26 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 21\n\nMarkdown Content:\nNeed for speed: Co-creating scenarios for climate neutral energy systems in Austria in 2040 \n\n# J. Schmidt  a, H. Mitter  a,d,* , M. Baumann  b, B. Boza-Kiss  c, D. Huppmann  c, S. Wehrle  a ,L. Zwieb  b, M. Klingler  a    \n\n> aInstitute of Sustainable Economic Development, University of Natural Resources and Life Sciences, Vienna, Austria\n> bAustrian Energy Agency, Austria\n> cInternational Institute for Applied Systems Analysis, Austria\n> dUniversity of Graz, Department of Environmental Systems Sciences, Austria\n\nA B S T R A C T \n\nThe Austrian government has pledged to achieve climate neutrality by 2040, but so far, there is a lack of consistent net-zero scenarios. Therefore, we integrate a comprehensive stakeholder with a techno-economic modelling process, coupling an energy and a power system model, to co-create qualitative scenario narratives and quantitative model-based scenarios for Austria ’s energy system, assuming all non-energetic emissions are eliminated. All four scenarios reach climate neutrality by 2040, but differ in terms of energy demand and trade in energy carriers. In the scenario narratives, variations in the local acceptance of renewables and of sufficiency lifestyles explain these differences. All scenario narratives emphasize that commitment of all societal actors is required to reach climate neutrality by 2040. We find that the quantitative model-based scenarios consistently point at far-ranging electrification of transport and heat supply, the buildup of renewables, and a sw\n\n## INCITE Technical Report on Innovative Techniques (TRIT)\nURL: https://publications.jrc.ec.europa.eu/repository/bitstream/JRC146559/JRC146559_01.pdf\nTitle: Request Rejected\n\nURL Source: https://publications.jrc.ec.europa.eu/repository/bitstream/JRC146559/JRC146559_01.pdf\n\nMarkdown Content:\nThe requested URL was rejected. Please consult with your administrator.\nYour support ID is: <4742023363446130477>\n\n[Go Back]\n\n\n## World Nuclear Industry Status Report 2025 (HTML version)\nURL: https://www.worldnuclearreport.org/World-Nuclear-Industry-Status-Report-2025-HTML-version\nWorld Nuclear Industry Status Report 2025 (HTML version) The Annual Reports WNISR Essential News WNISR Figures & Tables WNISR in the Media Who We Are Support What They Say… Our RSS feed WNISR on Twitter MENU Accueil The Annual Reports World Nuclear Industry Status Report 2025 26 November 2025 World Nuclear Industry Status Report 2025 ( HTML version) Foreword by Letizia Magaldi President, Kyoto Club, Rome, Italy By Mycle Schneider Independent Analyst, Paris, France Project Coordinator and Lead Author Julie Hazemann Director of EnerWebWatch, Paris, France Documentary Research, Modelling, and Datavisualization Phred Dvorak Independent Journalist Washington D.C., United States Contributing Author Emilio Godoy Independent Investigative Journalist Mexico Contributing Author Dmitry Gorchakov Nuclear Adviser, Bellona Foundation Vilnius, Lithuania Contributing Author Özgür Gürbüz Independent Consultant and Researcher Istanbul, Türkiye Contributing Author Bernd Hrdy Researcher, Institute for Safety and Risk Sciences, University of Natural Resources and Life Sciences (BOKU) Vienna, Austria Contributing Author Paul Jobin Associate Research Fellow, Institute of Sociology, Academia Sinica Taipei, Taiwan Contributing Author Timothy Judson Independent Consultant Syracuse, New York, United States Contributing Author Yuki Kobayashi Senior Research Fellow, Security Studies Program, Sasakwa Peace Foundation Tokyo, Japan Contributing Author Nikolaus Müllner Head, Institute for Safety and Risk Sciences, University of Natural Resources and Life Sciences ( BOKU) Chairman, International Nuclear Risk Assessment Group (INRAG) Vienna, Austria Contributing Author M.V. Ramana Simons Chair in Disarmament, Global and Human Security with the School of Public Policy and Global Affairs (SPPGA), Universit\n\n## Decarbonizing Low-Temperature Industrial Heat in the U.S. JEFFREY ...\nURL: https://energyinnovation.org/wp-content/uploads/Decarbonizing-Low-Temperature-Industrial-Heat-In-The-U.S.-Report-2.pdf\nTitle: Decarbonizing-Low-Temperature-Industrial-Heat-In-The-U.S.-Report-2.pdf\n\nURL Source: https://energyinnovation.org/wp-content/uploads/Decarbonizing-Low-Temperature-Industrial-Heat-In-The-U.S.-Report-2.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Wed, 13 Nov 2024 17:58:15 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 31\n\nMarkdown Content:\n# Decarbonizing Low-Temperature Industrial Heat in the U.S. \n\nJEFFREY RISSMAN ENERGY INNOVATION POLICY AND TECHNOLOGY LLC® 98 BATTERY ST. SUITE 202, SAN FRANCISCO, CA 94111 OCTOBER 2022 1 EI | DECARBONIZING LOW-TEMPERATURE INDUSTRIAL HEAT IN THE U.S. \n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY i,ii \n\nU.S. industrial facilities use low-temperature heat (up to 165 degrees Celsius) in numerous manufacturing processes, accounting for approximately 35 percent of industrial process heat demand. Low-temperature industrial heating produced 171 million metric tons of carbon dioxide (CO 2) in 2021 (3.5 percent of total U.S. energy-related CO 2 emissions), equivalent to the annual emissions from 37 million gasoline-powered cars, 22 million homes, or 430 natural gas-fired power plants. There exist a range of technology options to reduce these emissions. Energy efficiency, material efficiency, and circular economy measures can reduce the demand for low-temperature industrial heat and are important complements to zero-carbon heat generation. Direct electrification using heat pumps is the most efficient and cost-effective method of supplying low-temperature heat for industrial processes. Heat pumps can be several times more efficient than combustion technologies because they move heat like a refrigerator or air conditioner, rather than creating heat from their input energy, and they do not lose heat in combustion exhaust gases. Other zero-carbon solutions, such as burning electrolytic hydrogen, burning sustainably gr\n\n## Defining National Fossil Fuel Phase Out Roadmaps\nURL: https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/240400/2/Defining%20national%20fossil%20fuel%20phase%20out%20roadmaps-%20a%20Colombian%20case%20study%20foreword.pdf\nTitle: Defining National Fossil Fuel Phase Out Roadmaps\n\nURL Source: https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/240400/2/Defining%20national%20fossil%20fuel%20phase%20out%20roadmaps-%20a%20Colombian%20case%20study%20foreword.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Fri, 24 Apr 2026 10:23:34 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 115\n\nMarkdown Content:\nDefining National Fossil \n\n# Fuel Phase Out Roadmaps \n\n## A Colombian Case Study \n\nAlejandro Romero Prieto, Elliott Johnson, Prof Steve Pye, Dan Zhang, \n\nProf Keywan Riahi, Dr Setu Pelz, Dr Shanta Barley, Prof Piers Forster \n\nApril 2026 Foreword by \n\n# Prof. Rachel Kyte \n\n# Special Representative \n\n# for Climate, UK \n\n# Government \n\n## The first summit on Transitioning \n\n## Away from Fossil Fuels (TAFF) \n\n## could not come at a more \n\n## important time. \n\nThe war in Iran is threatening fossil supplies \n\nglobally. Airports are running out of jet fuel and \n\nvital fertilizer shipments are being missed. The \n\nprice of diesel and petrol has risen sharply, \n\nthreatening livelihoods. Depending on fossil fuels \n\nto such an extent brings enormous risk – for \n\nsecurity, economies and the environment. \n\nThis roadmap for Colombia – a collaboration \n\nbetween Colombian and UK experts – shows a \n\nclear path to economic and societal benefit. It \n\nbuilds off examples of successful implementation \n\nof policies in other countries. For example, the UK \n\nclosed its last coal-fired power station last year. \n\nThe roadmaps show that investment is needed to \n\ndeliver change, but this investment brings rapid \n\nemission reduction and clear economic benefits \n\nby the 2030s. \n\nWe can learn from each other and the successes \n\naround the world. Climate councils such as \n\nthe UK Climate Change Committee and its \n\ncounterpart within the Colombian ministries have \n\nvery important roles to play\n\n## EPA Climate Pollution Reduction Grant Planning Grant First ...\nURL: https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-03/connecticut-state-pcap.pdf\nTitle: EPA Climate Pollution Reduction Grant Planning Grant First Deliverable: A Priority Climate Action Plan\n\nURL Source: https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-03/connecticut-state-pcap.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Fri, 15 Mar 2024 20:27:28 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 183\n\nMarkdown Content:\nDRAFT March 1, 2024 \n\n# EPA Climate Pollution Reduction Grant Planning Grant First Deliverable: \n\n# A Priority Climate Action Plan \n\n# Fourteen near-term, implementation-ready climate action measures aligned with Connecticut’s Governor’s Council on Climate Change recommendations and sector-specific climate plans to inform applications for the EPA Climate Pollution Reduction Grant Phase 2 Implementation Grant \n\n# Department of Energy and Environmental Protection \n\n# State of Connecticut DRAFT March 1, 2024 \n\n1\n\n# Table of Contents \n\nTable of Contents ............................................................................................................... 1\n\nAcronyms, Abbreviations and Definitions ....................................................................... 4 \n\nI. Overview: The Climate Pollution Reduction Grant Climate Action Plans .................. 5 \n\nA. A Priority Climate Action Plan – implementation-ready climate action measures for federal funding ......................................................................................................... 5\n\nB. The role of the 14 climate action measures within Connecticut’s climate change landscape ........................................................................................................................ 7\n\nC. A Comprehensive Climate Action Plan – a roadmap to meet Connecticut’s climate goals ................................................................................................................. 10\n\n## Nuclear Energy - Supply Chain Deep Dive Assessment US ...\nURL: https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2024-12/Nuclear%2520Energy%2520Supply%2520Chain%2520Report%2520-%2520Final%5B1%5D.pdf\nTitle: Department of Energy\n\nURL Source: https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2024-12/Nuclear%20Energy%20Supply%20Chain%20Report%20-%20Final[1].pdf\n\nWarning: Target URL returned error 404: Not Found\n\nMarkdown Content:\n[Skip to main content](https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2024-12/Nuclear%20Energy%20Supply%20Chain%20Report%20-%20Final[1].pdf#main-content)\n\n![Image 1](https://www.energy.gov/themes/custom/energy_gov/assets/img/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere's how you know\n\nHere's how you know\n\n![Image 2: Dot gov](https://www.energy.gov/themes/custom/energy_gov/assets/img/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**  \n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![Image 3: Https](https://www.energy.gov/themes/custom/energy_gov/assets/img/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**  \n A **lock** (  ) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n_[![Image 4: Home](https://www.energy.gov/themes/custom/energy\\_gov/img/logo-image.png)](https://www.energy.gov/ \"Home\")_\n\n*   Policy & Priorities\n    *   Main Menu\n    *   Policy & Priorities[View all Policy & Priorities](https://www.energy.gov/topics/priorities) \n    *   [Increase American Energy Production Expanding domestic energy resources to support national needs and economic growth.](https://www.energy.gov/topics/increase-american-energy-production \"Expanding domestic energy resources to support national needs and economic growth.\") \n    *   [Energy Innovation Driving research and development to shape the future of energy systems and technologies.](https://www.energy.gov/topics/energy-innovation \"Driving research and devel\n\n## Energy Transition Outlook 2025 – Maritime Forecast to 2050\nURL: https://www.oceanspacemedia.com/files/2025/09/10/DNV_Maritime_Forecast_to_2050_2025_report.pdf\nTitle: \n\nURL Source: https://www.oceanspacemedia.com/files/2025/09/10/DNV_Maritime_Forecast_to_2050_2025_report.pdf\n\nMarkdown Content:\n\n\n\n## Defossilization, Not Decarbonization: - mgt.tum.de\nURL: https://www.mgt.tum.de/fileadmin/mgt.tum.de/downloads/FottnerFroehlingBuetheEtAl_AutomotiveLCA-WhitePaper_ForDigitalDistribution.pdf\nTitle: FottnerFroehlingBuetheEtAl_AutomotiveLCA-WhitePaper_ForDigitalDistribution.pdf\n\nURL Source: https://www.mgt.tum.de/fileadmin/mgt.tum.de/downloads/FottnerFroehlingBuetheEtAl_AutomotiveLCA-WhitePaper_ForDigitalDistribution.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Wed, 02 Sep 2026 09:46:29 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 92\n\nMarkdown Content:\nDefossilization, Not Decarbonization: \n\n# Toward a Life Cycle-Based Standard \n\n# for Automotive Greenhouse Gas Regulation \n\nJohannes Fottner *, Magnus Fröhling *, Tim Büthe *\n\nFrancesco Ferraresi, Anna M. Fischer, Vellah K. Kigwiru, Jana Schirmeister, Chris Schönherr, Salome Varamishvili \n\n# White Paper \n\n1 September 2026 Commissioned by the President of the Technical University of Munich (TUM), Prof. Dr. Thomas Hofmann  \n\n> *\n\nJoint corresponding authors. Affiliations and contact information, see verso. I\n\n# Executive Summary \n\nThe EU regulates the climate performance of passenger cars at a single point: the tailpipe. Everything else \n\nthat determines a vehicle’s true climate footprint – like the carbon embedded in its steel, aluminum and battery, the fossil or renewable origin of the energy that powers it, the materials recovered at the end of its life – remains invisible to the metric that decides regulatory compliance. At the same time, the EU regulatory framework lacks precision, focusing on \"decarbonization\" rather than what really matters: defossilization, i.e., the reduction of fossil carbon drawn from geological storage. As a result, this White Paper argues, the regulatory framework has a structural blind spot with significant economic and political consequences: a regulation that cannot see a contribution to defossilization cannot reward it, and what regulation does not reward, industry will not finance at scale. \n\nThe consequence is a systematic \n\n## THE 2026 GLOBAL ENERGY AGENDA | Atlantic Council\nURL: https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/the-2026-global-energy-agenda.pdf\nTitle: \n\nURL Source: https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/the-2026-global-energy-agenda.pdf\n\nMarkdown Content:\n\n\n\n## Need for speed: Co-creating scenarios for climate neutral energy ...\nURL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301421524005135\nTitle: Need for speed: Co-creating scenarios for climate neutral energy systems in Austria in 2040\n\nURL Source: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301421524005135\n\nMarkdown Content:\n[Skip to main content](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301421524005135#main)[Skip to article](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301421524005135#article)\n\n[![Image 2: Elsevier logo](https://www.sciencedirect.com/shared-assets/24/images/elsevier-non-solus-new-grey.svg)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/)\n\n*   [Journals & Books](https://www.sciencedirect.