pest Business University, Alkotmány utca 9-11, 1054 Budapest, Hungary\n\n4\n\nFaculty of Science, Centre of Environmental Sciences, Eötvös Loránd University, Pázmány Péter Sétány 1/A, 1117 Budapest, Hungary\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe global rise in population and advancement in civilization have led to a substantial increase in energy demand, particularly in the industrial sector. This sector accounts for a considerable proportion of total energy consumption, with approximately three-quarters of its energy consumption being used for heat processes. To meet the Paris Agreement goals, countries are aligning policies with international agreements, and companies are setting net-zero targets. Upstream emissions of the Scope 3 category refer to activities in the company’s supply chain, being crucial for achieving its net-zero ambitions. This study analyzes heating solutions for the supply chain of certain globally operating companies, contributing to their 2030 carbon-neutral ambition. The objective is to identify current and emerging heating solutions from carbon dioxide equivalent (CO 2 e) impact, economic, and technical perspectives, considering regional aspects. The methodology includes qualitative and quantitative su\n\n## Decarbonizing industrial heat to face climate change | Arthur D. ...\nURL: https://www.adlittle.com/en/insights/report/decarbonizing-industrial-heat-face-climate-change\nTitle: Decarbonizing industrial heat to face climate change | Arthur D. Little\n\nURL Source: https://www.adlittle.com/en/insights/report/decarbonizing-industrial-heat-face-climate-change\n\nMarkdown Content:\n[](https://www.adlittle.com/en/insights/report/decarbonizing-industrial-heat-face-climate-change)\n\n###### DATE\n\nFebruary 2024\n\nIs thermal energy storage the missing link in Europe’s industrial decarbonization strategy?\n\n![Image 1: Decarbonizing industrial heat to face climate change](https://www.adlittle.com/sites/default/files/reports/ADL_Decarbonizing%20Heat_header_image_1605x662.jpg)\n\n### Executive Summary\n\n**Decarbonizing industrial heat consumption is crucial to reduce carbon emissions and mitigate the effects of climate change. Currently, the top five heat-demanding industries in the EU — chemical & petrochemical; nonmetallic minerals; food, beverage & tobacco; paper pulp, paper & graphic arts; and iron & steel — account for more than 80% of heat consumption and urgently need decarbonization strategies.**\n\nDirect and indirect electrification, carbon capture, utilization, and storage (CCUS), and biomass are among the main technologies for decarbonizing industrial heat. Thermal energy storage (TES) is a potential solution for direct electrification, particularly for industries with process temperatures that reach up to 500°C. Key strengths of TES technology include storage opportunities (i.e., ability to store heat for later use and to integrate non-programmable renewable systems such as photovoltaics and wind), ease of installation, modularity, and cost competitiveness.\n\nUtilities can seize the opportunity and be pioneers in this market by focusing on testing the technology and building partnerships with vendors to speed up commercial deployment. Additionally, the\n\n## Characterization of Operational Flexibility Resources and Evaluation ...\nURL: https://freidok.uni-freiburg.de/files/237239/ySOr1W5MbMbl0SPa/Dissertation_NW_final.pdf\nTitle: Dissertation_NW_final.pdf\n\nURL Source: https://freidok.uni-freiburg.de/files/237239/ySOr1W5MbMbl0SPa/Dissertation_NW_final.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Tue, 03 Sep 2024 13:23:59 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 199\n\nMarkdown Content:\n# Characterization of Operational Flexibility Resources and Evaluation of Potential Utilization in Distributed Energy Systems \n\n# Dissertation \n\nzur Erlangung des Doktorgrades der Technischen Fakult¨ at der Albert-Ludwigs-Universit¨ at Freiburg \n\nvorgelegt von \n\n# Natapon Wanapinit \n\nangefertigt am Fraunhofer-Institut f¨ ur Solare Energiesysteme ISE und Institut f¨ ur Nachhaltige Technische Systeme INATECH \n\n# 2023 Dekan: Prof. Dr. Roland Zengerle \n\nPr¨ ufungskommission \n\nErstgutachterin: Prof. Dr. Anke Weidlich Zweitgutachter: Prof. Dr. Hans-Martin Henning Vorsitz: Prof. Dr. Moritz Diehl Beisitz: Prof. Dr. Niklas Hartmann Datum der m¨ undlichen Pr¨ ufung: 04. Mai 2023 “Every few hundred years throughout Western history, a sharp transformation has occurred. In a matter of decades, society altogether rearranges itself—its world view, its basic values, its social and political structures, its arts, its key institutions. Fifty years later a new world exists. And the people born into that world cannot even imagine the world in which their grandparents lived and into which their own parents were born. Our age is such a period of transformation. Only this time the transformation is not confined to Western society and Western history. Indeed, one of the fundamental changes is that there is no longer a Western history or a Western civilization. There is only world history and world civilization.” Peter F. Drucker (1992) Abstract \n\nThe rapid expansion of intermittent renewables and the electrification of heating and transport sectors are essential to decarboniz\n\n## Corporate Energy and Emissions Management Plan: 2019-2024\nURL: https://www.burlington.ca/en/home-property-and-environment/resources/Environment/Environmental-Initiatives/Energy-and-City-Operations/Burlington-CEEMP-2019-2024.pdf\nTitle: Burlington Corporate Energy and Emissions Plan\n\nURL Source: https://www.burlington.ca/en/home-property-and-environment/resources/Environment/Environmental-Initiatives/Energy-and-City-Operations/Burlington-CEEMP-2019-2024.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Thu, 28 Jul 2022 17:40:34 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 100\n\nMarkdown Content:\nCorporate Energy and Emissions Management Plan: 2019-2024 Corporate Energy and Emissions Management Plan 2019-2024 \n\n# The City of Burlington BURLINGTON CORPORATE ENERGY AND EMISSIONS MANAGEMENT PLAN: 2019-2024 iii \n\n> \n\n# Contents \n\nExecutive summary  ................................................................................................................................ ........... v \n\nPurpose  ................................................................................................................................ ........................ v \n\nScope and methods  ................................................................................................................................ .. v \n\nMajor assumptions  .................................................................................................................................. vi \n\nStatus of data  ........................................................................................................................................... vii \n\nMain findings  ............................................................................................................................................ vii \n\nConclusions and recommendations  ................................................................................................ ...... x\n\nBackground and context  ....................................................................................................................\n\n## MONTENEGRO FOURTH NATIONAL COMMUNICATION ON ...\nURL: https://cdn.climatepolicyradar.org/navigator/MNE/2024/montenegro-biennial-transparency-report-btr1-national-communications-nc4_9f6fc91e019a1fb40c5ef1ec9eafea69.pdf\nTitle: MONTENEGRO FOURTH NATIONAL COMMUNICATION ON CLIMATE CHANGE\n\nURL Source: https://cdn.climatepolicyradar.org/navigator/MNE/2024/montenegro-biennial-transparency-report-btr1-national-communications-nc4_9f6fc91e019a1fb40c5ef1ec9eafea69.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Wed, 16 Apr 2025 09:14:04 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 386\n\nMarkdown Content:\nFourth National Communication and the First Biennial Transparency Report of Montenegro to the UNFCCC \n\n# 2024 \n\nMontenegro, \n\nMinistry of Ecology, Sustainable \n\nDevelopment, and Northern \n\nRegion Development i\n\nPodgorica, January 2025 ii \n\n# Acknowledgment \n\nThe Fourth National Communication and the First Biennial Transparency Report of Montenegro to the UNFCCC were prepared by the Ministry of Ecology, Sustainable Development and North Region Development  through the project funded by  Environment Facility (GEF) and supported by the United Nations Development Program (UNDP). \n\nShort excerpts from this publication may be reproduced without alteration and without prior permission, provided the source is appropriately cited. iii \n\nLIST OF EXPERTS \n\nPROJECT TEAM \n\nJovana Drobnjak – Project Manager \n\nAndrijana Ćuković – Project Assistant \n\nNina Mihailović – Junior Expert \n\nJelena Ječmenica – Junior Expert \n\nIvan  Šikmanović  – Junior Expert \n\nEDITOR \n\nSeth Landau and Marta Calore - ECA \n\nINTRODUCTION \n\nSeth Landau and Marta Calore - ECA \n\nNATIONAL CIRCUMSTANCES \n\nJelena Peruničić \n\nSanja Elezović  \n\n> Juan Luis Martin Ortega\n\n– Gauss International \n\nGHG INVENTORY \n\nDuško Mrdak – EPA Montenegro \n\nNataša Vojinović  – MONSTAT \n\nMITIGATION  \n\n> Juan Luis Martin Ortega\n\n– Gauss International \n\nADAPTATION  \n\n> Juan Luis Martin Ortega\n\n– Gauss International \n\nMirjana Ivanov – Institute for Hydrometeorology and Seismology \n\nBen Andrews – Adapt40 iv \n\nTABLE O\n\n## Poland energy outlook, 2026 & beyond | Arthur D. Little\nURL: https://www.adlittle.com/en/insights/report/poland-energy-outlook-2026-beyond\nTitle: Poland energy outlook, 2026 & beyond | Arthur D. Little\n\nURL Source: https://www.adlittle.com/en/insights/report/poland-energy-outlook-2026-beyond\n\nMarkdown Content:\n[](https://www.adlittle.com/en/insights/report/poland-energy-outlook-2026-beyond)\n\n###### DATE\n\nDecember 2025\n\nSecuring stability as the green transition accelerates\n\n![Image 1: Poland energy outlook, 2026 & beyond](https://www.adlittle.com/sites/default/files/reports/ADL_Poland%20energy_hive_header_1605x662.png)\n\n### Executive Summary\n\nPoland stands at a critical juncture in its energy transition, driven by the urgent need to replace coal-dependent generation while meeting rapidly growing electricity demand. The 2022 energy crisis, which saw Polish electricity prices peak at €270/MWh, starkly illustrated the economic risks of relying on carbon-intensive energy sources, as EU carbon prices continue their upward trajectory toward €130/t CO2 by 2030.\n\nThe country faces an unprecedented electrification wave, with net electricity consumption projected to surge from 154 terawatt hours (TWh) in 2024 to 210-230 TWh by 2040 — a 1.9%-2.5% annual growth rate driven primarily by transport electrification, heat pump adoption, industrial automation, and digital infrastructure expansion. This fundamentally reshapes Poland’s energy requirements and underscores the inadequacy of the current coal-dominated mix, which makes Poland’s electricity system the EU’s most carbon-intensive, at 666 grams carbon dioxide equivalent (CO2e)/kWh.\n\n### STRATEGIC PATHWAYS & GENERATION MIX\n\nAs described in this report, Poland’s energy future centers on three potential scenarios with different nuclear deployment timelines. All three scenarios feature renewables as the dominant source by 2040, accounting for over 70% of installed capaci\n\n## African Green Hydrogen Report | PtX Hub\nURL: https://ptx-hub.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/African-Green-Hydrogen-Report-2025.pdf\nTitle: African-Green-Hydrogen-Report-2025.pdf\n\nURL Source: https://ptx-hub.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/African-Green-Hydrogen-Report-2025.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Thu, 24 Jul 2025 08:23:38 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 198\n\nMarkdown Content:\n# Introduction African Green Hydrogen Report \n\n# Potential to Power: \n\n# Advancing Green Hydrogen Across Africa \n\n> GREEN HYDROGEN\n> SOUTH AFRICA 3African Green Hydrogen Report\n\nAs a federally owned enterprise, GIZ supports the German Government in achieving its objectives in the field of international cooperation for sustainable development. \n\nPublished by: \n\nDeutsche Gesellschaft für Internationale Zusammenarbeit (GIZ) GmbH Dag-Hammarskjöld-Weg 1–5 65760 Eschborn Germany www.giz.de \n\nContact persons: \n\nMthokozisi Ndlela, Technical Advisor H2.SA, mthokozisi.ndlela@giz.de Florian Schmitt-Syren, Advisor H2-diplo, florian.schmitt@giz.de Joyce Kabui, Liaison, Africa Green Hydrogen Alliance, joyce.kabui@gh2.org \n\nAuthor/Responsible/Editor: \n\nGFA Consulting Group GmbH, Eulenkrugstr. 82, 22359 Hamburg, German Dr Theoneste Uhrorakeye Dr Joel Ruet Brent Cloete Jian Bani Lea Miram \n\nDesign/Layout: \n\nSaskia Nebel for civilisten.de \n\nPublication Date: \n\nMay 2025 This report is published jointly by the GIZ projects promoting a hydrogen economy in South Africa (implemented by GIZ on behalf of the Federal Ministry of Economic Cooperation and Development), H2-diplo (implemented by GIZ on behalf of the Federal Foreign Office and financed by the International Climate Initiative (IKI)) and PtX-Hub (implemented by GIZ on behalf of the Federal Ministry of Economic Affairs and Energy), in cooperation with the Africa Green Hydrogen Alliance. The opinions and recommendations expressed do not necessarily reflect the positions of GIZ or its commissioning institution\n\n## The 7th ASEAN Energy Outlook 2020 - 2050\nURL: https://asean.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/The-7th-ASEAN-Energy-Outlook-2022.pdf\nTitle: \n\nURL Source: https://asean.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/The-7th-ASEAN-Energy-Outlook-2022.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Wed, 26 Apr 2023 04:55:02 GMT\n\nWarning: This page maybe not yet fully loaded, consider explicitly specify a timeout.\n\nMarkdown Content:\n\n\n\n## HOW TO ACHIEVE A RAPID, FAIR, AND EFFICIENT ...\nURL: https://pure.iiasa.ac.at/id/eprint/19277/1/NAVIGATE-synthesis-report-compressed.pdf\nTitle: NAVIGATE-synthesis-report-compressed.pdf\n\nURL Source: https://pure.iiasa.ac.at/id/eprint/19277/1/NAVIGATE-synthesis-report-compressed.pdf\n\nPublished Time: Thu, 14 Dec 2023 15:46:30 GMT\n\nNumber of Pages: 72\n\nMarkdown Content:\nHOW TO ACHIEVE A RAPID, FAIR, AND EFFICIENT TRANSFORMATION TO NET ZERO EMISSIONS \n\n# Policy findings from the NAVIGATE project Elmar Kriegler, Jessica Strefler, Ramona Gulde,  Gerd Angelkorte, Nico Bauer, Luiz Bernardo Baptista, Olivier Dessens, Johannes Emmerling, Massimo  Tavoni, Panagiotis Fragkos, Dimitris Fragkiadakis, \n\nAnastasis Giannousakis, Céline Guivarch, Mathijs Harmsen, Florian Humpenöder, Julien Lefèvre, Alessio Mastrucci, Arooj Nawaz, Roberto Schaeffer, Isabela Schmidt Tagomori, Rik van Heerden, Hazel Pettifor, Bas van Ruijven, Detlef van Vuuren, Charlie Wilson, Sonia Yeh, Stéphane Zuber The NAVIGATE consortium ACKNOWLEDGEMENT \n\nThe research leading to these results has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 \n\nresearch and innovation programme under grant agreement No 821124. We would like to thank the members of the NAVIGATE Advisory Board for their guidance and advice on our research during the lifetime of the project. We would also like to thank the members of the policy, business, civil society and research communities who partici-pated in a series of NAVIGATE stakeholder and expert workshops for their contributions. \n\nRECOMMENDED REFERENCE \n\nKriegler, E., Strefler, J., Gulde, R. et al. 2023. \n\nHow to achieve a rapid, fair, and efficient transformation to net zero emissions – Policy findings from the NAVIGATE project. Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research. DOI: 10.48485/pik.2023.024 \n\nDISCLAIMER \n\nThe content of this report is the sole responsibility of the authors and can in no way be taken to refl\n\n## Publications - EPG - Energy Policy Group\nURL: https://www.enpg.ro/category/publications/\nTitle: Publications - EPG\n\nURL Source: https://www.enpg.ro/category/publications/\n\nMarkdown Content:\n### [A Regional Approach to Future-proofing CEE Industry](https://www.enpg.ro/a-regional-approach-to-future-proofing-cee-industry/ \"A Regional Approach to Future-proofing CEE Industry\")\n\nThe industrial base of Central and Eastern Europe is a key driver of the region’s economy, and its transformation will be essential for maintaining regional manufacturing in the long term. Transforming it will require concerted action by CEE states as well as a regional approach to exploit synergies, improve cost-effectiveness, and cooperate on mutually beneficial solutions. However, to date there has been insufficient action and regional coordination to enable an industrial transformation which capitalises on the region’s strengths. This publication presents a bird’s eye view of the region and its countries, reflects on the opportunities for cooperation, and puts forward concrete suggestions for anchoring the new industrial reality of Central and Eastern Europe in clean industry hubs fit for the future.​ Introduction and aims of this study: Central and Eastern Europe (CEE) has significant potential to become a pole of clean industrial production, but lacks the regional coordination essential for an efficient industrial transformation​ The current nationally siloed approach misses key opportunities to deliver industrial transformation, including coordinated infrastructure planning to leverage key resources across the region, and exploiting a regional market for industrial products​ The need for regional coordination comes in a time of pressure for CEE heavy industry to transform by decarbonising existing manufacturing and enabling new cleantech manufacturing, which will be essential to m\n\n## Energy Solutions for Decarbonization of Industrial Heat Processes\nURL: https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/17/22/5728\nTitle: &nbsp;\n\nURL Source: https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/17/22/5728\n\nWarning: This page maybe not yet fully loaded, consider explicitly specify a timeout.\n\nMarkdown Content:\n\n",
